[node name="ViewModel" type="Node3D" parent="."]
editor_description = "The rifle you can see, and the ONLY node in the game that decides where the guard's hands are.

MatchController._attach_rifle parents this scene's root to the holder's Head with an IDENTITY transform, and Head/Camera is also at identity, so this node's local space IS camera space: (0,0,0) is the eye, -Z is where the guard is looking. Everything below is therefore read directly as \"right, down, forward of the eye\".

TUNABLES (this transform, in the order you will want to touch them):
  scale     0.75 uniform -- the model is 1.49 m end to end at 1.0, which eats
                            the frame at a 100 degree fov. Change this first.
  position  (0.30, -0.24, -0.38) -- right of the bore line, below the eye so you
                            look DOWN onto the scope, far enough forward that the
                            recoil pad (local +Z 0.34) clears the 0.05 m near
                            plane with room to spare. Nearest vertex to camera
                            sits at about 0.13 m.
  rotation  (-6, -9, 0) degrees, Godot's YXZ euler -- muzzle nudged down and to
                            the right. Without it a 100 degree fov foreshortens
                            the barrel until the brake sits on the crosshair,
                            which is exactly what the tower must not have. With
                            it the brake projects to roughly (+0.17, -0.23) in
                            normalised device coordinates: lower right, crosshair
                            and the whole ring clear.

The nine-degree yaw costs nothing visually. WeaponProfile.tracer_segment_start is
12 m, so the drawn streak begins twelve metres down the muzzle-to-impact line and
never touches the barrel; the barrel's own heading is decoration.

This transform is the HIP pose -- the rest state at the bottom of both systems
hung off it below. Ads/RifleAds blends this node between here and its own
aimed pose as WeaponOptic zooms, and Recoil/RifleRecoil kicks it on Rifle.fired
and decays it back to exactly wherever Ads currently has it -- hip, aimed, or
mid-transition between the two. Edit the hip numbers here as always; edit the
aimed pose on Ads. Flash, bolt, sway and bob are still NOT authored here.

If you hand-edit the twelve floats above rather than dragging the node: they are
the basis ROWS, not the basis columns. Writing the columns ships the inverse
rotation, which yaws the barrel back toward the crosshair -- 10.3 degrees off the
aim axis instead of 24 -- and tests/test_weapon.gd fails on exactly that."
transform = Transform3D(0.740766, 0.012264, -0.116683, 0, 0.745891, 0.078396, 0.117326, -0.077431, 0.736708, 0.3, -0.24, -0.38)

[node name="Model" parent="ViewModel" instance=ExtResource("4_rifle_model")]
editor_description = "assets/models/rifle.glb, untouched and at identity: 456 triangles, one flat material, no UVs, no skin, no animation. Instanced rather than inherited so the model stays a pure import -- reimporting it must never silently rewrite a transform the game depends on.

Its own axes are already the ones the game wants: bore on y=0, shot line down local -Z, scope directly above the origin, stock back toward +Z, muzzle at local (0, 0, -1.150). Bounds are x +/-0.12, y -0.29 (bipod feet) to +0.18 (scope top), z -1.15 to +0.34.

Every offset lives on the parent instead of here, so Muzzle below and this mesh are moved by one transform and cannot drift apart."

[node name="Muzzle" type="Marker3D" parent="ViewModel"]
editor_description = "Where a shot LOOKS like it came from. Rifle.muzzle points here.

A sibling of Model rather than a child of it, at the model's own muzzle coordinate (0, 0, -1.150), so ViewModel's transform carries both: retune the position, rotation or scale above and the tracer origin follows the barrel end automatically, with no second number to remember.

This is NOT where shots go. Rifle._resolve_shot takes its ray origin and direction from aim_source -- the holder's Camera -- so the shot line is the eye's and there is no muzzle-to-crosshair parallax to explain away. Moving this marker moves the tracer and nothing else. tests/test_weapon.gd holds that line down."
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, -1.15)

[node name="HumanTrigger" type="Node" parent="." node_paths=PackedStringArray("weapon")]
script = ExtResource("2_weapon_input")
weapon = NodePath("..")

[node name="Recoil" type="Node" parent="." node_paths=PackedStringArray("rifle", "view_model", "pose_source")]
editor_description = "Kicks ViewModel on Rifle.fired and settles it back onto wherever Ads currently has it -- hip, aimed, or mid-transition, via pose_source. Listens to the signal, never Input, so a bot-driven guard's shot kicks the model exactly as a human's does. Every number is on WeaponProfile's Recoil group (Rifle.profile), not here -- see scripts/weapon/rifle_recoil.gd."
script = ExtResource("5_rifle_recoil")
rifle = NodePath("..")
view_model = NodePath("../ViewModel")
pose_source = NodePath("../Ads")

[node name="Ads" type="Node" parent="." node_paths=PackedStringArray("view_model")]
editor_description = "Blends ViewModel between its authored hip pose above and its own aimed pose as WeaponOptic zooms in and out -- the same progress value the FOV change itself uses, read off optic rather than clocked separately, so the raise and the zoom arrive together. optic is wired at runtime by MatchController._attach_rifle, the same way aim_source is, because the optic lives on the holder's Head and this scene is reparented onto whichever head holds the tower. Does not write ViewModel itself -- Recoil above is the sole writer, via pose_source -- so a shot fired while aimed recoils from the aimed pose and the two never fight over the node on the same frame. The aimed pose is exported on this node's own Aimed Pose group, not authored here as a transform, so it can be nudged in the inspector the same way the hip pose is nudged above. Appended LAST: scenes/bot/tower_shooter.tscn addresses this scene's children by ordinal index and inserting a node ahead of this one would silently re-point HumanTrigger's override."
script = ExtResource("6_rifle_ads")
view_model = NodePath("../ViewModel")

[node name="ScopeVignette" parent="." node_paths=PackedStringArray("ads", "trigger") instance=ExtResource("7_scope_vignette")]
editor_description = "The optic, drawn on the screen instead of modelled on the gun. A dark border closes in around a clear centre as the raise finishes, which -- with the field of view already narrowed -- is the whole of the scope: the scope on the model stays the solid brick tools/modelling/rifle_build.py makes it, because Ryan settled that it does not need to be see through.\n\nWired to the two siblings above and nothing else. Ads answers what the transition is doing, so the border runs on the same progress the raised pose and the FOV do and cannot drift from them; HumanTrigger answers whether the HUMAN is holding this rifle, which is the gate that keeps the vignette off a prisoner's, a spectator's, a ghost's and a bot-held tower's screen. MatchController needs no new line for either.\n\nAppended LAST, after Ads, for the reason Ads records above: scenes/bot/tower_shooter.tscn addresses this scene's children by ordinal index (HumanTrigger is 1) and INSERTING a node would silently re-point that override. Writes nothing but its own overlay -- no camera, no FOV, no ViewModel transform -- so deleting it leaves the aim, the zoom and the shot line exactly as they are."
ads = NodePath("../Ads")
trigger = NodePath("../HumanTrigger")
